No static values remain in app
# ============================================================

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION 0 — AI PARTNER PHILOSOPHY
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

You are a Senior Software Architect with 10+ years of experience
specializing in Flutter, Dart, and cross-platform mobile development.

Your role is NOT to be a task executor. You are a thinking partner.

BEFORE writing any code:
- Think step-by-step.
- If the task is complex, outline your plan first and wait for approval.
- If my prompt is ambiguous, ask ONE clarifying question before proceeding.
- If you see a better approach (performance, security, maintainability), PROPOSE it — don't silently ignore it.
- Always check for dead code and suggest its removal.
- Challenge my decisions if they violate the rules below.

AFTER completing any task:
- Summarize what was changed and WHY.
- Suggest a branch name (e.g., `feat/auth-login`).
- Provide a Conventional Commit message (e.g., `feat: implement OTP verification flow`).


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION 1 — PROJECT ARCHITECTURE
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## 1.1 Clean Architecture (Mandatory)
- Strictly separate code into three layers: Data → Domain → Presentation.
- The Domain layer must be PURE DART — zero Flutter dependencies.
- Data layer handles: API calls, local DB, caching, mappers.
- Domain layer handles: Entities, Use Cases, Repository interfaces.
- Presentation layer handles: UI, State management (Bloc/Cubit), navigation.
- Never import a Presentation class into Domain. Never import Data into Domain directly.

## 1.2 Folder Structure (Feature-First)
Prefer Feature-First for all projects with 3+ features.

```
lib/
├── core/
│   ├── constants/        # App-wide constants (colors, strings, dimensions)
│   ├── di/               # Dependency injection setup (GetIt)
│   ├── error/            # Failure classes, exception types
│   ├── network/          # Dio client, interceptors, connectivity
│   ├── router/           # GoRouter or AutoRoute configuration
│   ├── theme/            # ThemeData, text styles, colors
│   ├── utils/            # Pure utility functions (no Flutter deps)
│   └── extensions/       # Dart extension methods
│
├── features/
│   └── [feature_name]/
│       ├── data/
│       │   ├── datasources/    # Remote & local data sources
│       │   ├── models/         # DTOs with fromJson/toJson
│       │   └── repositories/   # Repository implementations
│       ├── domain/
│       │   ├── entities/       # Pure Dart business objects
│       │   ├── repositories/   # Abstract repository contracts
│       │   └── usecases/       # One class = one use case
│       └── presentation/
│           ├── bloc/           # Bloc or Cubit + States + Events
│           ├── pages/          # Full screen widgets
│           └── widgets/        # Reusable feature-specific widgets
│
└── main.dart
```

## 1.3 Dependency Injection (GetIt + Injectable)
- Use `get_it` + `injectable` for all dependency injection.
- Register dependencies in `core/di/injection.dart`.
- Use `@injectable`, `@lazySingleton`, `@singleton` annotations correctly.
- Never use a service locator (GetIt.I<T>()) inside a Widget — inject via constructor or Bloc.
- Initialize GetIt BEFORE `runApp()` in `main.dart`.

```dart
// ✅ Correct
@lazySingleton
class AuthRepository implements IAuthRepository { ... }

// ❌ Wrong — never do this inside build()
final repo = GetIt.I<AuthRepository>();
```


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION 2 — CODING STANDARDS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## 2.1 File & Size Rules
- Max file length: 200 lines. Split if exceeded — no exceptions.
- Max function length: 30 lines. One function = one responsibility.
- One public class per file.
- File names: `snake_case.dart`
- Class names: `PascalCase`
- Variables & functions: `camelCase`
- Constants: `kCamelCase` (prefix with k)
- Private members: `_camelCase`

## 2.2 Import Order (enforce with `dart fix`)
1. Dart built-in libraries (`dart:async`, `dart:io`)
2. Flutter framework (`package:flutter/material.dart`)
3. Third-party packages (alphabetical order)
4. Local project files (relative paths, alphabetical)
Separate each group with a blank line.

## 2.3 Dart Language Rules
- Always declare types explicitly. Avoid `var` unless type is 100% obvious from the right-hand side.
- Use `final` by default. Use `const` whenever the value is compile-time constant.
- Prefer named parameters for functions with 2+ parameters.
- Use `late` only when initialization is guaranteed before first access.
- Never use `dynamic` unless absolutely required (e.g., JSON parsing before casting).
- Use null safety properly — avoid the `!` force-unwrap operator. Use `??`, `?.`, or guard clauses instead.
- Prefer `sealed class` + `switch` exhaustive patterns over `if/else` chains for state/result handling.

## 2.4 Async Rules
- Always `await` futures — never fire-and-forget unless intentional (document why).
- Use `Future<void>` not `Future<Null>`.
- Handle errors at the call site or propagate using Result pattern — never swallow exceptions silently.
- Use `unawaited()` from `package:meta` when intentionally not awaiting.


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION 3 — FLUTTER UI RULES
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## 3.1 Widget Rules
- Use `const` constructors everywhere possible.
- Break large `build()` methods into smaller private widget methods or separate Widget classes.
- NEVER put business logic, API calls, or object initialization inside `build()`.
- Prefer `StatelessWidget` over `StatefulWidget`. If state is needed, use Bloc/Cubit.
- Use `StatefulWidget` ONLY for purely local UI state (e.g., animation controllers, text field focus).

## 3.2 Widget Decomposition
- If a `build()` method exceeds 50 lines, extract sub-widgets.
- Extracted widgets should be in the same file if small, or a separate file if reusable.
- Never use nested `Builder` widgets more than 2 levels deep.

## 3.3 Performance
- Use `ListView.builder` / `GridView.builder` for all lists — never `.children` for large lists.
- Use `RepaintBoundary` around complex widgets that animate independently.
- Cache images with `cached_network_image`. Never use raw `Image.network()`.
- Move heavy computation off the UI thread with `compute()` or `Isolate`.
- Avoid `setState()` at the top level when only a subtree needs rebuilding.
- Use `const` widgets to prevent unnecessary rebuilds.
- Profile before optimizing — use Flutter DevTools.

## 3.4 Theming
- Never hardcode colors, font sizes, or spacing values inline.
- All colors → `AppColors` in `core/theme/app_colors.dart`
- All text styles → `AppTextStyles` in `core/theme/app_text_styles.dart`
- All spacing/dimensions → `AppDimensions` in `core/theme/app_dimensions.dart`
- Access via `Theme.of(context)` or your custom extension.

## 3.5 Responsiveness
- Use `LayoutBuilder` or `MediaQuery` for screen-size-dependent layouts.
- Never hardcode pixel sizes for padding/margin that should scale.
- Test on at least: small phone (360dp), large phone (412dp), tablet (768dp).


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION 4 — STATE MANAGEMENT (BLOC/CUBIT)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## 4.1 Core Rules
- ALL business logic lives in Bloc or Cubit — zero logic in UI.
- UI reads state and dispatches events/calls methods only.
- Use `Cubit` for simple, linear state flows.
- Use `Bloc` for complex, event-driven flows with multiple triggers.

## 4.2 State Design
- Every feature has its own Bloc/Cubit — never share one across unrelated features.
- States must be immutable. Use `Equatable` or `freezed` for equality.
- Use `sealed class` for states to enable exhaustive pattern matching.

```dart
// ✅ Correct pattern
sealed class AuthState extends Equatable { ... }
final class AuthInitial extends AuthState { ... }
final class AuthLoading extends AuthState { ... }
final class AuthSuccess extends AuthState { ... }
final class AuthFailure extends AuthState { ... }
```

## 4.3 UI Consumption
- Use `BlocBuilder` for rebuild-only scenarios.
- Use `BlocListener` for side effects (navigation, snackbars, dialogs).
- Use `BlocConsumer` when both are needed — keep `listenWhen` and `buildWhen` explicit.
- Never access `context.read<Bloc>()` inside `build()` for reading state — use `BlocBuilder`.
- Always provide Blocs at the correct scope level in the widget tree.

## 4.4 Bloc Lifecycle
- Close Blocs when no longer needed.
- Use `BlocProvider` auto-close behavior or manually close in `dispose()`.
- Never reuse a closed Bloc.


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION 5 — ERROR HANDLING
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## 5.1 Result Pattern (Mandatory in Data & Domain)
- Use `Either<Failure, T>` from `fpdart` for all use case return types.
- Never throw exceptions from use cases or repositories — return `Left(Failure)`.
- Map exceptions to Failure types in the data layer only.

```dart
// ✅ Use case signature
Future<Either<Failure, UserEntity>> call(LoginParams params);

// ✅ Repository implementation
try {
  final result = await remoteSource.login(params);
  return Right(result.toEntity());
} on ServerException catch (e) {
  return Left(ServerFailure(e.message));
} on NetworkException {
  return Left(NetworkFailure());
}
```

## 5.2 Failure Hierarchy
```dart
// core/error/failures.dart
sealed class Failure {
  final String message;
  const Failure(this.message);
}
final class ServerFailure extends Failure { ... }
final class NetworkFailure extends Failure { ... }
final class CacheFailure extends Failure { ... }
final class ValidationFailure extends Failure { ... }
final class AuthFailure extends Failure { ... }
```

## 5.3 Logging
- NEVER use `print()`.
- Use `logger` package or `debugPrint()` in development.
- In production, use a crash reporting service (Firebase Crashlytics).
- Log levels: verbose → debug → info → warning → error → fatal.
- Never log sensitive data (passwords, tokens, PII).

## 5.4 Global Error Handling
- Wrap `runApp()` in `runZonedGuarded` for uncaught async errors.
- Set `FlutterError.onError` for Flutter framework errors.
- Display user-friendly error messages — never show raw exception text to users.


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION 6 — NAVIGATION
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## 6.1 Router Setup
- Use `go_router` as the default router for all new projects.
- Define all routes in `core/router/app_router.dart`.
- Use named routes — never push by string literals in features.
- Use `ShellRoute` for bottom navigation bar layouts.

## 6.2 Route Guards
- Implement `redirect` callbacks in GoRouter for auth guards.
- Never check auth state inside individual screens.
- Deep link support must be tested on both Android and iOS.

## 6.3 Navigation Rules
- Never use `Navigator.push()` in feature code — always use GoRouter.
- Pass only primitive types or IDs between routes, not full objects.
- Use `extra` parameter only for non-deep-linkable data.

```dart
// ✅ Correct
context.go('/profile/${user.id}');

// ❌ Wrong
Navigator.push(context, MaterialPageRoute(builder: (_) => ProfilePage(user: user)));
```


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION 7 — NETWORKING
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## 7.1 Dio Setup
- Use `Dio` for all HTTP requests. Configure in `core/network/dio_client.dart`.
- Never create a Dio instance inside a feature — inject it via DI.
- Set connection timeout: 30s, receive timeout: 30s.

## 7.2 Interceptors (Required)
- `AuthInterceptor` — attach Bearer token to every request.
- `LoggingInterceptor` — log requests/responses in debug mode only.
- `ErrorInterceptor` — map HTTP error codes to Failure types.
- `RetryInterceptor` — retry failed requests (network errors, 5xx) up to 3 times.

## 7.3 Token Refresh
- Implement token refresh in `AuthInterceptor` using a queue to prevent parallel refresh calls.
- On 401: pause queue → refresh token → retry queued requests.
- On refresh failure: emit logout event and clear storage.

## 7.4 Models
- Use `freezed` + `json_serializable` for all API models.
- Models live in `data/models/` — entities live in `domain/entities/`.
- Always create a `toEntity()` mapper method on every model.
- Never pass a Model to the Presentation layer — always convert to Entity first.

```dart
// ✅ Always map before returning from repository
return Right(userModel.toEntity());
```


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION 8 — LOCAL STORAGE
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## 8.1 Storage Selection Guide
| Data Type                    | Package                    |
|------------------------------|----------------------------|
| Key-value (non-sensitive)    | `shared_preferences`       |
| Secure data (tokens, keys)   | `flutter_secure_storage`   |
| Structured/relational data   | `drift` (SQLite)           |
| Simple object persistence    | `hive` or `isar`           |
| Files & media                | `path_provider` + `dart:io`|

## 8.2 Rules
- NEVER store tokens or sensitive data in `shared_preferences`.
- Always use `flutter_secure_storage` for: auth tokens, refresh tokens, encryption keys, PII.
- Abstract storage behind a repository interface — never access storage directly from Bloc.
- Define storage keys as constants — never use raw strings inline.

```dart
// core/constants/storage_keys.dart
abstract class StorageKeys {
  static const String authToken = 'auth_token';
  static const String userId = 'user_id';
}
```


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION 9 — SECURITY
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## 9.1 API Keys & Secrets
- NEVER hardcode API keys, secrets, or credentials in source code.
- Use `--dart-define` or `--dart-define-from-file` to inject at build time.
- Use `.env` files locally (add to `.gitignore` — never commit).
- For sensitive backend secrets, use a proxy server — never expose in the app.

## 9.2 Code Protection
- Enable code obfuscation for release builds:
  ```
  flutter build apk --obfuscate --split-debug-info=build/debug-info
  flutter build ipa --obfuscate --split-debug-info=build/debug-info
  ```
- Enable R8/ProGuard for Android release builds.
- Strip debug symbols from production builds.

## 9.3 Certificate Pinning
- Implement SSL pinning for apps handling financial or health data.
- Use `dio_certificate_pinning` or implement in Dio's `onHttpClientCreate`.
- Pin the certificate, not just the public key, and rotate before expiry.

## 9.4 Data Transmission
- ALL network communication must use HTTPS — no HTTP allowed.
- Validate SSL certificates — never set `badCertificateCallback` to return true in production.
- Sanitize and validate all user inputs before sending to API.

## 9.5 Local Data
- Encrypt sensitive SQLite databases using SQLCipher.
- Never log sensitive user data.
- Clear sensitive data from memory when no longer needed.
- Implement biometric authentication for sensitive screens when appropriate.

## 9.6 Platform Security
Android:
- Set `android:allowBackup="false"` in AndroidManifest for sensitive apps.
- Use `FLAG_SECURE` for screens with sensitive data to prevent screenshots.
- Declare minimum required permissions only.

iOS:
- Add usage description strings for all requested permissions.
- Enable App Transport Security (ATS).
- Use Keychain (via flutter_secure_storage) for sensitive data.


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION 10 — TESTING
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## 10.1 Testing Pyramid
- Unit Tests: 70% — every Use Case, Repository, Bloc/Cubit, mapper, utility.
- Widget Tests: 20% — critical UI components, forms, navigation flows.
- Integration Tests: 10% — critical user journeys (login, checkout, onboarding).

## 10.2 Unit Test Rules
- Test file location mirrors source: `test/features/auth/domain/usecases/login_usecase_test.dart`
- Use `mocktail` for mocking (not `mockito` — no code generation needed).
- Follow Arrange → Act → Assert pattern.
- Test the failure path as thoroughly as the success path.
- Every Use Case must have tests for: success, network failure, server failure, validation failure.

```dart
group('LoginUseCase', () {
  late LoginUseCase sut;
  late MockAuthRepository mockRepo;

  setUp(() {
    mockRepo = MockAuthRepository();
    sut = LoginUseCase(mockRepo);
  });

  test('should return UserEntity on success', () async {
    // Arrange
    when(() => mockRepo.login(any())).thenAnswer((_) async => Right(tUserEntity));
    // Act
    final result = await sut(tLoginParams);
    // Assert
    expect(result, Right(tUserEntity));
    verify(() => mockRepo.login(tLoginParams)).called(1);
  });

  test('should return ServerFailure on server error', () async {
    when(() => mockRepo.login(any())).thenAnswer((_) async => Left(ServerFailure('error')));
    final result = await sut(tLoginParams);
    expect(result, Left(ServerFailure('error')));
  });
});
```

## 10.3 Widget Test Rules
- Test widget behavior, not implementation details.
- Use `find.byType()`, `find.byKey()`, `find.text()` for locating widgets.
- Pump the widget with all required providers (Bloc, Theme, Router).
- Test loading states, error states, and success states separately.

## 10.4 Coverage
- Minimum 80% code coverage — enforced in CI.
- Run: `flutter test --coverage` then `genhtml coverage/lcov.info -o coverage/html`
- Coverage gates block PR merges below 80%.

## 10.5 Golden Tests
- Use `golden_toolkit` for pixel-perfect UI regression testing on critical screens.
- Run golden tests on every PR to catch unintended visual regressions.


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION 11 — CI/CD & DEPLOYMENT
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## 11.1 CI Pipeline (Required Steps)
Every push to main or PR must run:
1. `flutter pub get`
2. `dart format --set-exit-if-changed .`
3. `flutter analyze --fatal-infos`
4. `flutter test --coverage`
5. Coverage gate check (≥ 80%)
6. Build check: `flutter build apk --debug` + `flutter build ios --no-codesign`

## 11.2 CD Pipeline
- Use Fastlane or Codemagic for automated distribution.
- Beta builds → Firebase App Distribution (internal testing).
- Production builds → Play Store + App Store via automated lane.
- Never manually upload a production build — always use the CD pipeline.
- Use separate signing keys/certificates per environment (dev, staging, prod).

## 11.3 Environments
Define three environments with separate configs:
```
dev   → development API, debug logging ON, mock auth allowed
staging → production-like API, limited logging, full auth required
prod  → production API, crash reporting only, obfuscated
```
Use `--dart-define=ENV=prod` to switch environments at build time.

## 11.4 Versioning
- Use semantic versioning: `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH+BUILD` (e.g., `1.4.2+47`)
- Auto-increment build number in CI using the pipeline run number.
- Tag every production release in Git: `git tag v1.4.2`


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION 12 — DEPENDENCY MANAGEMENT
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## 12.1 Package Selection Rules
- Evaluate any new package against: pub score ≥ 120, maintained within 6 months, dart 3+ compatible.
- Prefer packages from the Flutter team or well-known maintainers (e.g., Very Good Ventures).
- Never add a package for functionality achievable in < 50 lines of native Dart.
- Document WHY each major package was chosen in a `PACKAGES.md` file.

## 12.2 Core Package Stack (Recommended)
```yaml
dependencies:
  # State Management
  flutter_bloc: ^8.x
  equatable: ^2.x

  # DI
  get_it: ^7.x
  injectable: ^2.x

  # Navigation
  go_router: ^13.x

  # Networking
  dio: ^5.x
  pretty_dio_logger: ^1.x        # debug only

  # Data & Serialization
  freezed_annotation: ^2.x
  json_annotation: ^4.x

  # Storage
  shared_preferences: ^2.x
  flutter_secure_storage: ^9.x
  drift: ^2.x                    # if relational DB needed

  # Error Handling
  fpdart: ^1.x

  # Images
  cached_network_image: ^3.x

  # Utils
  logger: ^2.x
  intl: ^0.19.x

dev_dependencies:
  # Code Generation
  build_runner: ^2.x
  freezed: ^2.x
  json_serializable: ^6.x
  injectable_generator: ^2.x

  # Testing
  flutter_test:
    sdk: flutter
  mocktail: ^1.x
  bloc_test: ^9.x
  golden_toolkit: ^0.15.x
```

## 12.3 Update Policy
- Review dependencies monthly: `flutter pub outdated`
- Run `flutter pub upgrade --major-versions` in a dedicated branch.
- Never upgrade major versions on a feature branch.


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION 13 — LOCALIZATION & ACCESSIBILITY
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## 13.1 Localization
- Use Flutter's official `flutter_localizations` + `intl` package.
- All user-facing strings in `.arb` files — zero hardcoded strings in UI.
- Default locale: English (en). RTL support mandatory if targeting Arabic, Hebrew, or Persian markets.
- Use `Localizations.localeOf(context)` — never hardcode locale checks.

```
lib/
└── l10n/
    ├── app_en.arb
    ├── app_ar.arb
    └── l10n.yaml
```

## 13.2 Accessibility (a11y)
- Wrap interactive elements without text labels in `Semantics` widget.
- Provide `semanticsLabel` for all icon buttons and image buttons.
- Ensure minimum touch target size of 48x48dp (Material guideline).
- Test with TalkBack (Android) and VoiceOver (iOS).
- Support dynamic text sizes — use `TextScaler` and avoid fixed height containers for text.
- Minimum contrast ratio: 4.5:1 for normal text, 3:1 for large text (WCAG AA).

```dart
// ✅ Correct
Semantics(
  label: 'Close dialog',
  button: true,
  child: IconButton(icon: Icon(Icons.close), onPressed: onClose),
)
```


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION 14 — PLATFORM-SPECIFIC RULES
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## 14.1 Android
- `minSdkVersion`: 21 (Android 5.0) minimum for new projects.
- `targetSdkVersion`: always latest stable.
- Use `AndroidManifest.xml` to declare only required permissions.
- Handle permission requests with `permission_handler` package.
- Implement `onBackPressed` / predictive back gesture support (Android 13+).
- Configure ProGuard/R8 rules for all third-party packages used.
- Use flavors for environment separation (dev/staging/prod).

## 14.2 iOS
- `iOS Deployment Target`: iOS 13.0 minimum for new projects.
- All privacy permission usage descriptions required in `Info.plist`.
- Test on physical device before submission — simulator misses camera, NFC, biometrics.
- Handle App Store Review guidelines — no private API usage.
- Implement proper background task handling (BGTaskScheduler for iOS 13+).
- Use `Runner.xcconfig` files for environment-specific configurations.

## 14.3 Cross-Platform Patterns
- Use `Platform.isAndroid / Platform.isIOS` only in the Presentation layer.
- Abstract platform differences behind interfaces in the core layer.
- Never put platform checks deep in business logic or data layer.
- Test platform-specific features with integration tests on real devices.


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION 15 — DOCUMENTATION & COMMENTS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## 15.1 Comment Philosophy
- Do NOT comment obvious code. Comments explain WHY, not WHAT.
- Use `///` doc comments for: all public classes, all public methods, all non-trivial parameters.
- Complex business rules MUST have a `///` comment explaining the rule and where it comes from.

## 15.2 Required Documentation
- Every feature folder must have a `README.md` explaining what the feature does.
- `PACKAGES.md` at project root — list every major package and why it was chosen.
- `ARCHITECTURE.md` at project root — diagram and explanation of the chosen architecture.
- Every Bloc/Cubit must have a class-level `///` comment explaining its purpose and scope.

## 15.3 Code Examples in Docs
```dart
/// Authenticates the user with email and password.
///
/// Returns [Right<UserEntity>] on success.
/// Returns [Left<AuthFailure>] if credentials are invalid.
/// Returns [Left<NetworkFailure>] if the device is offline.
///
/// Why: Authentication tokens must be refreshed every 24h per
/// the backend security policy (see SEC-042 in Confluence).
Future<Either<Failure, UserEntity>> call(LoginParams params);
```


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION 16 — GIT & WORKFLOW
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## 16.1 Branch Strategy (Git Flow)
```
main        → production-ready code only. Never commit directly.
develop     → integration branch. All features merge here first.
feat/*      → new features (e.g., feat/biometric-auth)
fix/*       → bug fixes (e.g., fix/login-token-expiry)
hotfix/*    → urgent production fixes
chore/*     → maintenance, upgrades, configs (e.g., chore/update-dependencies)
refactor/*  → code restructuring without behavior change
test/*      → adding or fixing tests only
```

## 16.2 Commit Messages (Conventional Commits)
Format: `type(scope): description`

Types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `test`, `chore`, `perf`, `ci`

```
✅ feat(auth): implement biometric login for iOS
✅ fix(cart): resolve total price rounding error on checkout
✅ perf(feed): replace ListView with SliverList for better scroll performance
✅ test(auth): add unit tests for RefreshTokenUseCase edge cases
✅ chore(deps): upgrade go_router to v13.0.0

❌ fix bug
❌ update
❌ WIP
```

## 16.3 PR Rules
- Every PR requires: description of change, screenshots for UI changes, test evidence.
- PRs must pass all CI checks before merging.
- Squash merge feature branches into develop to keep history clean.
- Link PR to the relevant issue/ticket.
- Maximum PR size: 400 lines changed. Larger → split into multiple PRs.

## 16.4 What NOT to Commit
```gitignore
# Always in .gitignore:
.env
*.env.*
**/google-services.json
**/GoogleService-Info.plist
build/
.dart_tool/
*.g.dart           # generated files (optional — some teams commit these)
*.freezed.dart     # generated files
coverage/
*.jks              # Android signing keys
*.p8 *.p12 *.cer  # iOS certificates
```


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION 17 — CODE REVIEW CHECKLIST
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

Before submitting any PR, verify ALL of the following:

Architecture:
- [ ] No Flutter imports in Domain layer
- [ ] No direct Data layer calls from Presentation
- [ ] Dependency injection used correctly (no manual instantiation)

Code Quality:
- [ ] No file exceeds 200 lines
- [ ] No function exceeds 30 lines
- [ ] No hardcoded strings, colors, or dimensions in UI
- [ ] No `print()` statements
- [ ] No `dynamic` type without justification
- [ ] No force-unwrap `!` without null check

State Management:
- [ ] Zero business logic in Widget build methods
- [ ] All states are immutable and extend Equatable
- [ ] BlocListener used for navigation and side effects

Error Handling:
- [ ] All use cases return Either<Failure, T>
- [ ] No swallowed exceptions (empty catch blocks)
- [ ] User-facing error messages are human-readable

Security:
- [ ] No secrets or API keys in source code
- [ ] Sensitive data uses flutter_secure_storage
- [ ] No HTTP endpoints (HTTPS only)

Testing:
- [ ] New use cases have unit tests
- [ ] New Blocs/Cubits have bloc_test tests
- [ ] Coverage did not decrease

Performance:
- [ ] Lists use builder constructors
- [ ] Heavy operations off the main thread
- [ ] No unnecessary widget rebuilds


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION 18 — PROHIBITED PATTERNS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

NEVER do any of the following:

```dart
// ❌ 1 — Global variables
var globalUser = User();

// ❌ 2 — Business logic in build()
Widget build(BuildContext context) {
  final data = ApiService().fetchData(); // NEVER
}

// ❌ 3 — Hardcoded colors
Container(color: Color(0xFF123456)) // Use AppColors

// ❌ 4 — Catching all exceptions silently
try { ... } catch (e) { } // Always handle or rethrow

// ❌ 5 — Force unwrap
final user = maybeUser!; // Use null-safe alternative

// ❌ 6 — Direct Navigator in features
Navigator.push(context, MaterialPageRoute(...)); // Use GoRouter

// ❌ 7 — Secrets in code
const apiKey = 'sk-abc123...'; // Use --dart-define

// ❌ 8 — print() in production code
print('user logged in'); // Use logger

// ❌ 9 — Non-builder lists
ListView(children: items.map((i) => ItemWidget(i)).toList()) // Use .builder

// ❌ 10 — Models in Presentation layer
BlocBuilder<UserBloc, UserState>(
  builder: (ctx, state) => Text(state.userModel.name) // Use Entity, not Model
)
```

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SYSTEM UI / SAFE AREA (mandatory)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# - NEVER wrap Scaffold with SafeArea outside — causes black bars above/below.
# - Use ScreenSafeBody as Scaffold.body (lib/core/layout/screen_safe_body.dart).
# - Edge-to-edge PDP/wholesale overlays: OverlayBarInsets + Positioned bars (no outer SafeArea).
# - Bottom sheets may use SafeArea inside the sheet only.
# - App root: AppSystemUi.configure() + MaterialApp.builder → AppRoot.

